    {"words": "Sejam envergonhados e desonrados, * os que buscam", "problem": "The comma separates the predicate from its subject, which Portuguese punctuation does not allow; the asterisk already gives the pause. Minor, but it looks like a translation.", "alternative": "Sejam envergonhados e desonrados * os que buscam a minha alma."}
  ]},
  {"id": "69:4", "fails": ["noRhyme"], "remarks": [
    {"words": "Voltem para trás, e corem, * os que me querem mal", "problem": "'Voltem' here and 'Voltem-se' in 4b render the same avertántur with two different forms, so the repetition the Latin makes is blurred. There is also a faint echo 'corem / querem' across the mediant.", "alternative": "Voltem-se para trás, e corem, * os que me querem mal."}
  ]},
  {"id": "69:4b", "fails": ["plain", "native", "sayable"], "remarks": [
    {"words": "imediatamente", "problem": "A long, bureaucratic adverb (six syllables) that weighs down the colon; statim is a short word and wants a short word.", "alternative": "Voltem-se logo, corando, * os que me dizem: Bem feito, bem feito!"},
    {"words": "Que bom, que bom", "problem": "In Brazilian ears 'que bom' is warm and approving, so the mockery of Euge, euge is lost; it sounds like a friend being pleased. 'Bem feito!' is the native cry of gloating over another's misfortune and keeps the doubled exclamation.", "alternative": "os que me dizem: Bem feito, bem feito!"}
  ]},
  {"id": "69:5", "fails": ["sayable"], "remarks": [
    {"words": "alegrem-se em vós todos os que vos buscam", "problem": "'em vós todos' parses first as 'in all of you', and the reciter has to back up; the word 'todos' belongs to the subject but glues to 'vós'.", "alternative": "Em vós exultem e se alegrem todos os que vos buscam: * e digam sempre os que amam a vossa salvação: Engrandecido seja o Senhor."}
  ]},
  {"id": "69:6", "fails": ["plain"], "remarks": [
    {"words": "Deus, auxiliai-me", "problem": "'auxiliai-me' is heavy and clerical at the most urgent cry of the psalm; the short final colon wants a short, strong verb. 'socorrei-me' is the plainer faithful word for ádjuva.", "alternative": "Eu, porém, sou necessitado e pobre: * Deus, socorrei-me."}
